# GBin — Ultra-fast GPU-accelerated metagenomic binning

![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.10%2B-blue)
![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-green)
![Platform](https://img.shields.io/badge/platform-WSL2%20%7C%20Linux%20%2B%20NVIDIA-orange)
![Tests](https://img.shields.io/badge/tests-81%20passing-brightgreen)

**GBin** recovers genomes (metagenome-assembled genomes, MAGs) from metagenomic
assembly contigs. It is a **hybrid** binner that runs the heavy lifting on a
single consumer GPU:

- a self-supervised **variational autoencoder (VAE)** embeds contigs from
  tetranucleotide composition + per-sample abundance;
- **single-copy marker genes** (pyrodigal + pyhmmer) add cannot-link constraints
  and decontaminate bins;
- the latent space is clustered **on the GPU** — a robust pure-PyTorch iterative
  medoid by default, with optional cuGraph Leiden;
- optional **[CheckM2](https://github.com/chklovski/CheckM2)** integration for
  accurate final QC and **CheckM2-guided decontamination**.

It is inspired by [VAMB](https://github.com/RasmussenLab/vamb) (VAE + iterative
medoid clustering) and [SemiBin2](https://github.com/BigDataBiology/SemiBin)
(self-supervised learning + marker constraints), and pushes more of the pipeline
onto the GPU: k-mer counting, mixed-precision (bf16) training, and graph
clustering.

> Status: actively developed; the full pipeline is implemented and covered by an
> 81-test CPU suite. GPU/RAPIDS paths are validated on WSL2 + NVIDIA.

## Highlights

- **GPU end-to-end** — k-mer counting, VAE training (bf16), clustering, and the
  decontamination splitter all run on the GPU; no GPU is *required* (everything
  also runs on CPU), but it is built for one.
- **Maps reads for you** — point gbin at FASTQ reads and it runs strobealign /
  minimap2 to compute coverage; or feed it BAMs or precomputed `--aemb` TSVs.
- **Two quality estimators** — a fast built-in single-copy-gene scorer that runs
  during binning, plus opt-in CheckM2 for accurate, publishable numbers.
- **CheckM2-guided refinement** — split the bins CheckM2 flags as contaminated
  (on the GPU) and keep a split only if CheckM2 confirms it actually improves
  quality.
- **Cached stages** — composition, mapping, abundance, markers, and the latent
  are cached, so re-running with different model/clustering settings is cheap.

## Requirements

- **OS/GPU:** WSL2 or Linux with an NVIDIA GPU. Developed on an RTX 5070 Ti
  (16 GB, Blackwell / sm_120, CUDA 12.8); works on any CUDA GPU, and on CPU
  (slowly). Memory budget: < 16 GB VRAM, < 32 GB RAM for typical assemblies.
- **Python** ≥ 3.10 (the conda env uses 3.12).
- **External tools** (installed by `environment.yml`): strobealign / minimap2 /
  samtools (read mapping), and optionally CheckM2 (final QC).

## Installation

The recommended setup is a single conda env (no RAPIDS, so it solves fast and
includes CheckM2). **Use `mamba`/libmamba** — the classic conda solver is slow on
bioinformatics stacks.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/RosieCaTT/GBin.git && cd GBin
mamba env create -f environment.yml        # or: conda env create -f environment.yml
conda activate gbin
# Blackwell GPUs need CUDA 12.8+; if the bundled torch lacks sm_120 kernels:
pip install --force-reinstall torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128
pip install -e .
checkm2 database --download                # one-time ~3 GB CheckM2 DB (optional, for --checkm2)
```

The default clusterer (`medoid`) needs no RAPIDS. For the optional `--cluster
leiden` fast path, add cuML + cuGraph afterwards (see
[docs/install.md](docs/install.md) §2.3). Full setup, the manual recipe, a
separate-env CheckM2 fallback, and troubleshooting are in
[docs/install.md](docs/install.md).

## Quick start

Abundance (coverage) is the strongest binning signal. If you have **reads**, gbin
maps them for you — nothing to precompute:

```bash
# One sample's paired reads
gbin bin -i contigs.fna.gz --reads s1_R1.fq.gz,s1_R2.fq.gz -o gbin_out

# Multiple samples (best results) — repeat --reads, or use a manifest TSV.
# Mapping ALL samples to the assembly gives strong co-abundance signal and is the
# single biggest lever for separating closely related genomes.
gbin bin -i contigs.fna.gz \
    --reads s1_R1.fq.gz,s1_R2.fq.gz \
    --reads s2_R1.fq.gz,s2_R2.fq.gz \
    -o gbin_out
gbin bin -i contigs.fna.gz --reads-tsv samples.tsv -o gbin_out   # name<TAB>R1<TAB>R2 per line

# Long reads (Nanopore/PacBio)
gbin bin -i contigs.fna.gz --reads ont.fq.gz --mapper minimap2 --mapper-preset map-ont -o gbin_out

# Accurate final QC + CheckM2-guided decontamination
gbin bin -i contigs.fna.gz --reads-tsv samples.tsv -o gbin_out --checkm2-refine
```

Already have coverage? Skip mapping:

```bash
gbin bin -i contigs.fna.gz -a aemb/*.tsv -o gbin_out     # strobealign --aemb TSVs
gbin bin -i contigs.fna.gz --bamdir bams/ -o gbin_out    # sorted BAM files
```

## How it works

```
reads ─ map (strobealign --aemb │ minimap2) ─→ coverage           io/mapping.py
FASTA ─ composition (GPU k-mers) ─────────────→ TNF [N,103]        features/composition.py
      ─ abundance (reads │ BAM │ aemb TSV) ───→ cov  [N,S]         io/abundance.py
      ─ markers (pyrodigal + pyhmmer) ────────→ SCGs / contig      markers/find.py
normalize (GPU) → VAE (bf16) → latent [N,32]                       features/, model/
cluster (medoid │ cuGraph Leiden) → labels                        cluster/
refine (SCG split) [+ CheckM2-guided split] → write bins + report cluster/refine.py, qc/, io/write.py
```

## Bin quality & CheckM2

gbin reports two estimates of completeness / contamination:

- **Built-in (on by default with markers):** counts ~104 universal single-copy
  genes. Fast enough to run *inside* the decontamination loop and as a quick flag
  in `bins_info.tsv`. It is a coarse heuristic and can under-estimate
  contamination.
- **CheckM2 (opt-in, accurate):** an ML-based estimate. Add `--checkm2` to
  `gbin bin`, or run it later on an existing output:

  ```bash
  gbin qc -o gbin_out                       # CheckM2 on gbin_out/bins
  ```

  Numbers are merged into `bins_info.tsv` as `checkm2_completeness` /
  `checkm2_contamination` (next to the built-in columns); the native report is
  saved under `gbin_out/checkm2/`. CheckM2 ships in the same conda env; or keep it
  separate and pass `--checkm2-bin /path/to/checkm2`.

- **CheckM2-guided refinement (opt-in):** `--checkm2-refine` (or `gbin qc -o
  gbin_out --refine -i contigs.fna.gz`) splits the bins CheckM2 flags as
  contaminated, trying several GPU KMeans partitions per bin, and **keeps a split
  only if CheckM2 confirms it improves the DAS-Tool score**
  (`completeness − 5·contamination`) by a margin — so cosmetic "peel one contig"
  splits are rejected. It recurses into still-contaminated children. The split
  math runs on the GPU; CheckM2 itself is CPU-bound (DIAMOND), so this is slower
  than plain `--checkm2`. After refinement the native report under `checkm2/` is
  rewritten to match the final bins. Tune with `--checkm2-refine-min-contamination`,
  `--checkm2-refine-min-gain`, `--checkm2-refine-max-k`, `--checkm2-refine-iters`.

> Tip: a heavily contaminated bin that won't split is usually two genomes that
> overlap in the latent space. The most effective fix is **more samples** — co-
> abundance across samples separates them far better than any post-hoc split.

## Outputs

In `gbin_out/`:

| Path | Contents |
|------|----------|
| `bins/*.fna` | one FASTA per bin |
| `bins_info.tsv` | per-bin completeness / contamination / size / N50 (+ `checkm2_*` if run) |
| `contig_bins.tsv` | contig → bin assignment |
| `checkm2/` | CheckM2's native report (if `--checkm2*`) |
| `cache/` | cached composition / mapping / abundance / markers / latent |

## Subcommands

`gbin bin` runs the whole pipeline. Individual stages (each cached) are also
exposed: `composition`, `abundance`, `markers`, `cluster`, and `qc` (CheckM2 on an
existing output). Run `gbin <cmd> --help` for options.

## Tests

```bash
pytest tests -q          # 81 CPU tests (synthetic data; CheckM2/mappers mocked)
```

The GPU/RAPIDS code paths run on `device='cpu'` in tests, so correctness is
covered on any machine; only raw GPU speed/VRAM needs a real card to observe.

## Citation

If gbin is useful in your work, please cite it (see [CITATION.cff](CITATION.cff))
and the tools it builds on: VAMB, SemiBin2, CheckM2, pyrodigal, and pyhmmer.

## License

[MIT](LICENSE) © Ruochen Chao.

GBin reuses two data assets from **VAMB** (MIT): the 256→103 TNF projection kernel
(`gbin/data/kernel.npz`) and the single-copy marker HMM set (`gbin/data/marker.hmm`).
It also builds on the ideas and ecosystems of SemiBin2, CheckM2, pyrodigal, and
pyhmmer — thanks to their authors.
